## AI-Assisted

## Per-account polyinstantiation of shared, world-writable temporary
## directories using pam_namespace. Drop-in; parsed after the libpam-modules
## default /etc/security/namespace.conf.
##
## Goal: a user must not see, read, race, or predict another account's temp
## files in /tmp, /var/tmp, /dev/shm. libpam-tmpdir only sets $TMPDIR and is
## bypassed by any program that hardcodes /tmp - pam_namespace gives a real
## private mount, so hardcoded /tmp is isolated too.
##
## Method 'user': ONE instance per account, shared across ALL of that account's
## PAM sessions - interactive login, the systemd --user manager, and cron - so
## they agree on /tmp and /dev/shm (no systemd --user split; background-service
## temp files are isolated too). This requires pam_namespace to run in the
## non-interactive stack as well, so the pam-config is NOT interactive-only and
## carries unmnt_remnt (see namespace-security-misc).
##
## Columns: polydir  instance_prefix  method[:flags]  excluded_users
##   - /tmp, /dev/shm instances live on a dedicated nosuid,nodev,noexec tmpfs
##     (/run/pam-namespace, run-pam\x2dnamespace.mount): RAM-backed, wiped at
##     reboot, and the hardened mount options are inherited through the bind
##     (the 'user' method cannot set mntopts itself, unlike 'tmpfs'). noexec
##     mirrors the system's global mount-hardening policy for /tmp and /dev/shm.
##   - /var/tmp uses an on-disk per-account instance (FHS: persists at reboot).
##   - root and adm are excluded so system/daemon logins keep the shared dirs.
##
## iscript on /tmp recreates the X11/ICE socket dirs inside a freshly created
## instance (see pam-namespace-init). $HOME is deliberately NOT polyinstantiated
## - that would hide the user's own data and only makes sense on SELinux MLS.
##
## https://www.kicksecure.com/wiki/Dev/Strong_Linux_User_Account_Isolation
## man namespace.conf

/tmp       /run/pam-namespace/tmp-inst/   user:iscript=/usr/libexec/security-misc/pam-namespace-init   root,adm
/var/tmp   /var/tmp/tmp-inst/             user   root,adm
/dev/shm   /run/pam-namespace/shm-inst/   user   root,adm
